# PART 6 — REAL BACKEND DOCUMENT PROCESSING

Demo data and mock generators must not be required for these features.

The converters must continue working after all demo data has been removed.

They must be integrated with the Java backend and PostgreSQL-compatible application architecture.

Do not attempt to implement production-grade PDF extraction and image-aware Excel processing entirely through fragile frontend-only mock logic.

### Recommended Java backend technology

The backend team may use appropriate maintained libraries.

Suggested technologies include:

**PDF processing**
- Apache PDFBox for PDF text and image extraction.
- Apache Tika for document detection and extraction.

**Scanned document OCR**
- Locally hosted Tesseract OCR with appropriate language configuration.
- Layout-aware table and text processing.
- Suitable image-processing libraries for scanned documents.

**Excel generation and parsing**
- Apache POI or another suitable XLSX implementation that genuinely supports embedded images and workbook drawings.

The selected implementation must preserve document structure and correctly associate values, images, questions and answer options.

Do not rely on SheetJS alone if the selected configuration cannot reliably preserve or extract embedded Excel images.

Equivalent libraries may be used, but the functionality and image round-trip correctness are mandatory.

### Offline and institutional data handling

Prefer local/on-premises document processing wherever possible.

Do not silently upload confidential student information or examination question papers to external AI/OCR services.

Any external document-processing service requires explicit institutional approval.

### Temporary files

- Store temporary uploads securely.
- Associate every conversion with the authenticated HOD.
- Restrict conversion access to that HOD and authorised administrators.
- Provide appropriate retention and deletion policies.
- Avoid permanent storage of unnecessary original documents.
- Keep actual student and question records in their authorised backend workflows.

